US-126537 - 6e [TAR] Billing Files - Disbursements Tab - Special-Case Layouts (Customers 1063, 1114, 2141, 2259)

Description

Synopsis:
Elevate customers require payment billing files to update their own systems and LNET system should generate billing files that include all items reported under a TAR job. The system should ensure the file structure aligns with each clients' specifications.

As a loan servicing user on the TAR Disbursements tab, I want to export a billing file for customers 1063, 1114, 2141, and 2259, so that each receives a .txt file in their unique required layout containing all reported TAR job items.

Background
These four customers do not fit any of the patterns established in Stories B-D. Each requires its own distinct approach:

. 1063 uses a CTE and CONCAT to produce a compact 60-character row - the simplest layout in the entire set but structurally different from all others.
. 1114 sources data from yBillingFiles but outputs a completely different column structure: paired installments (1&3 together, 2&4 together) with the TBM ID as the contract field.
. 2141 produces the same output structure as 1114 but uses raw joins with MatchID instead of TBMID - requires its own SP rather than reusing yBillingFiles.
. 2259 requires two separate file sections: Query 1 produces a blank-installment header row (all installment fields are spaces), and Query 2 produces the standard Pattern 4 fixed-width rows. This is the only customer that requires a multi-section .txt file.

In Scope:

1. Read the customer profile to determine the clients who will receive this new report.
2. The report should be made available on the TAR disbursements tab [LNET].
3. Creation of billing file layouts for ~5 identified clients listed above. See attached
4. Ensuring all billing file exports match the required layout for each client. See attached
5. Export logic should include all items reported under the TAR job.

Out of Scope
1. Any customer not in this story's list
2. Changing the meaning or content of 2259's two-section structure - if the business intent of Section 1 is unclear, escalate before implementing


Acceptance Criteria

. AC1 - Existing ACs Inherited All ACs from Story A (profile gate, button visibility, all items included, .txt format) apply to all four customers in this story.
. AC2- Compact Concatenated Layout for 1063 Given customer 1063 exports a billing file, Then each row is exactly 60 characters: loan number right-padded with leading zeros to 16 chars, amount right-padded with leading zeros to 10 chars (decimal removed), due date formatted as MMddyy (6
chars), tax ID left-padded with spaces to 28 chars. No delimiter between fields.
. AC3 - Paired Installment Layout for 1114 Given customer 1114 exports a billing file, Then each row contains: payee/agency code, blank agency type, blank customer number, loan number, TBM ID as contract number, blank contract suffix, blank hit code, blank exemption code, state code, blank
county code, blank lien count, installment 1&3 amount, installment 2&4 amount, total tax amount, blank discounted total, tax ID, blank reference number, blank prior-year flag, blank service bureau, blank installment count, blank pay indicator, blank filler. When AmtToPay is null, amount fields contain
'0.00'.
. AC4 - MatchID-Based Layout for 2141 Given customer 2141 exports a billing file, Then the output structure matches AC3 (paired installment format), but the contract number field contains MatchID from Linkage (not TBMID), and total tax amount is only populated when IsCurrentTaxYear = 0 or
RType sequence is outside 1-4; otherwise null. WHAT DO WE DO WITH MATCHID
. AC5- Two-Section File for 2259 Given customer 2259 exports a billing file, Then the .txt file contains two sections in order: Section 1 (Query 1 rows): rows with blank customer number (''), blank state/county codes, and all installment fields as spaces - amount and reference number fields use
spaces, not zeros. Section 2 (Query 2 rows): standard Pattern 4 fixed-width rows (same structure as Story D) with real installment amounts, '1' as total installments, and actual SequenceNo. Both sections are present in every export regardless of data; Section 1 appears before Section 2 with no
separator line between them.



